  "strategy": "## Approach and rationale\n\nI approach this as a constrained optimization problem with a nonconvex objective. The current record for C\u2085 is 0.38085. My key observation is that the time domain [0,2] can be frequency-analyzed to identify which behavior contributes most to the overlap. By treating the problem as a filter design task, I map it to finding a step function that minimizes the maximum cross-correlation with its complement.\n\nThe core bottleneck is efficiently navigating the huge parameter space (n-pointable integers bounded between 0 and 1). Traditional gradient methods struggle due to:\n1. Nonseparable objective with moving maxima\n2. Time-costly correlation computations\n3. Sparse best recoveries\n\nMy strategy combines frequency-domain analysis with geometric constraints:\n- Recognizes only periodic solutions need be considered\n- Truncates sample space through discrete Fourier filtering\n- Uses phase maintenance of Fourier components\n- Explores structured parent-based neighborhoods\n\nThe mathematical proof of optimality is bypassed through:\n\n1. Implementation: Fourier-component projection\n2. Enforcement: Line search optimization around parent vectors\n3. Validation: Ensures parent feasibility via frequency inversion\n\nThis differs from the previous gradient-based approach by using:\n- More efficient function evaluations\n- Fewer local minima\n- Better global exploration\n- Higher verifiability\n\nThe approach is justified by:\n- Reduced evaluations through frequency domain\n- More stable convergence\n- Explicit frequency-constrained parents as fallbacks\n- Polynome-based frequency-interval mapping",
